<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed, notarized, direct-sale
    snapsift.app (not the App Store; not sandboxed — see the owner's ruling
    of 2026-08-02 in the repo's task history). Consumed by the family
    signing pipeline, cver-tools' mac-release/release.sh, via its
    --entitlements flag; this file does no signing itself.

    HARDENED RUNTIME IS NOT THE SANDBOX. They are two separate opt-ins with
    two separate entitlement vocabularies, and conflating them is what an
    earlier version of this file did: it reasoned that
    com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library "exists only to
    re-grant access to a SANDBOXED app", and therefore shipped zero keys.
    That is wrong for a hardened, non-sandboxed app. Apple's Hardened Runtime
    documentation lists Photos Library under RESOURCE ACCESS, alongside
    camera, microphone, location and address book — a section that has
    nothing to do with App Sandbox. Under the hardened runtime the system
    checks for the resource-access entitlement BEFORE it shows a TCC prompt;
    without it PHPhotoLibrary.requestAuthorization returns .denied with no
    dialog, which in this app reads as "the customer refused access" on the
    very first launch of the first paid build.

    So: com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library is REQUIRED
    here, and is the only key this app needs.

    It is not sufficient on its own. TCC also needs the purpose strings
    (NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription / NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription,
    already in app/Info.plist plus per-language InfoPlist.strings) to have a
    sentence to show the user. Entitlement = allowed to ask; usage string =
    what the prompt says. Both, or no access.

    com.apple.security.app-sandbox is deliberately ABSENT (that IS "not
    sandboxed"), and the hardened runtime's defaults (no JIT, no unsigned
    executable memory, no DYLD_* injection, no debugger attach, no loading of
    dylibs/frameworks not signed by the same Team ID, no sending Apple events
    to unrelated processes) are all left in force.

    Sparkle-specific finding, unchanged: Sparkle 2's sandbox-escape machinery
    — its Installer.xpc / Downloader.xpc XPC services and the
    temporary-exception.mach-lookup / temporary-exception.shared-preference
    entitlements that go with them (see Sparkle's own
    TestApplication/Sparkle-Test-App.entitlements) — exists only to let a
    SANDBOXED host reach outside its sandbox to install an update. snapsift
    is not sandboxed, so there is no boundary to cross: it runs its updater
    and relaunch helper (Autoupdate) as an ordinary child process. Adding
    those keys here would be inert cargo-culting.

    Library validation (the one hardened-runtime restriction Sparkle usually
    needs an exception for) is handled by SIGNING, not an entitlement:
    release.sh's `codesign --deep` re-signs Sparkle.framework and its nested
    Autoupdate.app with snapsift's OWN Developer ID Application identity
    before notarization, so every nested binary carries the same Team ID the
    hardened runtime checks for. com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
    is not needed unless that deep-sign step is ever skipped for Sparkle's
    nested code — if it is, add the key here and say why.

    Net result, as of 2026-09-16: exactly one key. Add another only when a
    real codesign/notarize/launch/TCC rejection names it, with a comment here
    explaining which mechanism needed it.

    STILL TO VERIFY before 1.0 ships: a signed+notarized build on a clean Mac
    must actually raise the Photos prompt. Nothing in this repo can prove that.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
